>
  <p>
    Every big city has a district the locals point visitors toward when they want to see 'the real place' — and in Shanghai, that district is the <strong>French Concession</strong>. From 1849 to 1943 this wedge of the city west of the Huangpu River was administered by France, and the legacy is architectural: plane-tree-lined avenues, cream-and-grey Art Deco apartment buildings, red-brick Shikumen lanes, and quiet villas that survived the city's skyscraper boom. Today it is Shanghai's most atmospheric quarter — the place to slow down, wander, and feel the city breathe.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unlike the <a href="/the-bund">Bund</a> or <a href="/shanghai-tower">Shanghai Tower</a>, the Concession has no single must-see monument. Its attractions are cumulative: a street corner, a doorway, a courtyard cafe, a shop selling vintage furniture. The right approach is to give it time, pick one or two anchor points, and let the streets do the rest. This guide gives you the anchors.
  </p>

  <h2>The best streets to wander</h2>
  <h3>Wukang Road</h3>
  <p>
    The Concession's most famous street is <strong>Wukang Road</strong>, anchored by the iconic <strong>Wukang Mansion</strong> — a 1924 wedge-shaped apartment block in the French Renaissance style that has become a top social-media photo spot. Start there, then walk the street's length through some of the best-preserved villa-lined blocks in Shanghai, passing the Former Residence of Ba Jin (the famous novelist) and the art galleries that now fill several old houses. The traffic-light island opposite Wukang Mansion is the classic photo angle; arrive early for it to yourself.
  </p>
  <h3>Anfu Road</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Anfu Road</strong> is the Concession's style spine — a short street of boutiques, vintage shops, indie fashion, and the city's best cafes packed into low-rise buildings. It is the centre of Shanghai's 'shopping while walking' culture, and it is genuinely delightful rather than mawkishly touristy. Pick one cafe, order a flat white, and watch the neighbourhood go about its day.
  </p>
  <h3>Fuxing Road and the villa lanes</h3>
  <p>
    Around <strong>Fuxing Road</strong> the streets open into grander residential blocks with hidden courtyards and gated lanes. Take any inviting-looking alley and you will likely find Shikumen houses, walled gardens, or a surprise boutique. The Former Residence of Sun Yat-sen on Xiangshan Road — a modest, perfectly preserved home where the revolutionary lived from 1918 — is a highlight and one of the few 'real sights' on this itinerary.
  </p>

  <h2>Xintiandi: the pretty re-creation</h2>
  <p>
    At the Concession's eastern edge, <strong>Xintiandi</strong> is a restored Shikumen district turned into a car-free open-air shopping and dining quarter. The red-brick lanes look authentically old — and were carefully rebuilt as a heritage-style development — but they are effortlessly pleasant: boutiques, terrace restaurants, and the <strong>Site of the First National Congress of the Communist Party of China</strong> (the small red-brick museum on the square is genuinely worth 30 minutes). Locals debate its authenticity; visitors tend to love it, and it is the easiest, most accessible taste of Concession-era architecture for first-timers.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting there</h2>
  <p>
    The Concession is served by several Metro lines. The most useful stops are <strong>South Shaanxi Road (Lines 1, 10, 12)</strong> — the hub in the heart of it — and <strong>Xintiandi (Lines 10, 13)</strong> for the eastern end. From either, the classic streets are a short walk. The area is flat, compact, and far better on foot or by shared bike than by taxi; rent a bike at any station and you can cover the whole quarter in an afternoon.
  </p>

  <h2>Food and coffee</h2>
  <p>
    This is Shanghai's premier cafe district — expect world-class flat whites at dozens of independent shops (Anfu Road alone has a handful worth queuing for) and plenty of bakeries and brunch spots. For a fuller Shanghainese meal, the Concession has some of the city's best local restaurants alongside its Western options. The classic <a href="/xiaolongbao">xiaolongbao</a> shops are a short ride away, and for the complete local food picture, see the <a href="/food">full Shanghai food guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How long to spend and how to combine it</h2>
  <p>
    Give the Concession a half day at minimum — a full day is easy to fill with wandering, cafes, and a museum or two. It combines naturally with a <a href="/the-bund">Bund</a> morning and a <a href="/yu-garden">Yu Garden</a> visit in the same day: both are a 15-minute Metro ride away. For a slower, calmer Shanghai experience than the skyscraper circuit, the Concession is the single best thing on the itinerary — the place where the city's European layers and its modern, coffee-obsessed present meet.
  </p>
